# A nineteen-metre charter boat, floating.
#
# **This scene exists to answer one question: does it sit upright and stay
# there.** Not whether it looks right — `assets/meshes/jib_vi_wide.obj` was
# judged on that separately — but whether the buoyancy model this engine has
# can hold a hull-shaped object level for minutes without capsizing, and
# whether it rights itself when pushed.
#
# **The pontoons are solved from the hull's own section curve, and that is the
# whole point of the scene.** `Buoyancy`'s default is four pontoons derived
# from the node's own size, at its horizontal corners, with radii summing to
# the body's volume. For a crate that is exactly right. For a boat it is badly
# wrong, because the node's bounding box is 19.07 x 9.96 x 6.60 m and **most of
# that box is superstructure and air** — the hull is the bottom 1.66 m of it.
# Four corner spheres sized to the whole box would displace several times what
# the hull does and the boat would ride on the surface like a cork, which is
# the failure the component's own documentation warns about in the neighbouring
# case.
#
# So sixteen pontoons — eight pairs, one pair per slab of equal displaced
# volume, each pair sized to reproduce that slab's displacement *and* its
# waterplane area. `../prefabs/jib_vi.loom` owns the derivation; three
# properties of it are worth having in front of you here:
#
#   - **Sixteen, not four**, because righting torque comes from the forces
#     landing at *different places*. Four give a boat almost no roll stiffness
#     at the beam, and a hull with no roll stiffness lies down at the first
#     asymmetry and never gets up — which is exactly the "capsizes for no
#     reason" this scene is built to catch.
#   - **z runs ±1.30 amidships down to ±0.73 forward**, each one the centroid
#     of that slab's own half-waterplane, because the hull narrows. Pontoons
#     wider than the hull would generate a righting moment the boat does not
#     have, which is the same lie as the cork, told the other way round.
#   - **Every sphere crosses the surface**, at immersions from 35% to 64% by
#     volume — none saturated, none dry. That is deliberate: a sphere fully
#     under displaces a constant volume no matter how the boat moves and
#     contributes **nothing** to stability. A sphere crossing the surface gains
#     and loses displacement as it rises and falls, and that change is the
#     restoring force. Pontoons buried below the waterline are a common way to
#     build a boat that floats and still capsizes.
#
# **The hull is `jib_vi_wide.obj`, and the beam is why every number here moved.**
# The first hull had a cabin ±2.52 m standing on a deck ±2.41 m — wider than the
# deck it sat on — so a player could stand on the aft deck or the foredeck and
# could not walk between them. Beam went 5.75 -> 6.60 m and the cabin half-width
# 2.52 -> 1.47, which buys a continuous side deck of 1.250 m clear at its
# narrowest, both sides, bow to stern. That is a gameplay requirement, but it is
# also the more faithful boat: a party boat sells rail space, and the reference
# photograph has anglers lined along the sides.
#
# **`mass` is measured, not scaled, and the scaling is the defect this file
# used to carry.** It read: the wide hull displaces 57.64 m^3 below the
# waterline against the narrow one's 50.0, a ratio of 1.152, so scale a guessed
# 38,000 kg by it and get 43,776. Neither input was a measurement of this hull,
# and the output was **24% short of what she actually displaces** — 13,860 kg
# light — while twelve spheres tuned to hold *that* weight up hid it by
# floating her at the right height for the wrong reason. `mass` is now
# **57,636 kg**, which is 57.636 m^3 of fresh water: `jib_vi_wide.obj`
# integrated below y = 0, three ways, agreeing to 0.02%. `../prefabs/jib_vi.loom`
# carries the provenance and the argument for the designed line over the
# painted one.
#
# **The scene first shipped with r = 0.945 at y = -0.6, and that is the failure
# the bullet above describes, written into the file it warns in.** Those
# displace the same 38.7 m^3 — the flotation arithmetic was right — but the
# spheres are **91% submerged**, leaving 11.6% reserve buoyancy, so they
# saturate almost immediately and stop generating any restoring force. The
# difference is not subtle and is measured below.
#
# **The wind is light on purpose.** A real sea would make a capsize ambiguous:
# a boat knocked over by a wave is a boat, a boat that lies down on flat water
# is a bug. `speed = 3.5` gives a surface with enough texture to see the hull
# respond without ever being the cause. The harder wave case belongs in its own
# scene once this one holds.
#
# The water is `simulation = "deterministic"` — the height-field tier, which
# carries force, the sim hash and `--assert` (ADR 0059 §1). A cinematic volume
# around a nineteen-metre hull is a separate experiment and a separate scene.
#
# **What this hull is measured to do.** Dropped from 0.4 m she settles at a
# mean of y = +0.0206 — 2.1 cm above the designed line — over ticks 1800-3900,
# and is still there at tick 36000, ten minutes in, having drifted 21 mm and
# bobbing 2.1-2.7 cm peak to peak on the wave texture. In still water (the same
# scene with `Wind.speed = 0`) she settles at **+0.02126 exactly and holds it**.
# The 2.1 cm is not this scene's arithmetic: it is a 2.5% buoyancy excess the
# engine adds whenever `damp_linear` is nonzero (the fixed step applies gravity
# before buoyancy, and the displacement-scaled damping rectifies that jitter
# into net lift — measured at the same 2.5% on an independent four-sphere
# calibration body, and extrapolating damp_linear -> 0 lands on y = 0 exactly).
# Do not tune `mass` to cancel it; the tune would go wrong the moment the
# integrator is fixed.
#
# **Trim at rest is +0.016°, in still water, and that number is the whole
# reason the pontoon stations are shifted 2.2994 m forward of the hull's own.**
# The engine has no way to say where a `RigidBody`'s centre of gravity is — it
# is the node origin — and this hull's centre of buoyancy is 2.2994 m aft of
# that. Left where the hull's volume is, she trims **8.78° by the head** and
# sinks 0.351 m. The prefab's `Buoyancy` comment has the argument.
#
# On the wave texture, trim and heel both peak at **0.63° and 0.67°** over
# ticks 1800-3900, so she is level in trim as well as in heel.
#
# **Heeled and released from the level settle height she rights herself from
# 87.5° and inverts from 87.8°** (still water, bisected to half a degree,
# released at rot_euler x = θ and read at tick 3600). On the twelve pontoons
# this file used to carry, the same rig on the same build gives 81.2° and
# 81.6°, so the corrected set is worth six degrees. Both numbers flatter the
# boat, because releasing at the *level* settle height starts her with more
# reserve than her own heeled equilibrium would; say which release you mean if
# this line ever becomes an assertion. Once over she floats hull-up
# indefinitely — the inverted state is stable, which is correct for an enclosed
# superstructure, though nothing here models one.
#
# **Neither vanishing angle is a claim about the real boat's range of
# stability**, and this is worth saying because the old header's "~75°" read
# like one. A pontoon proxy has no deckhouse and no downflooding point; what
# these numbers measure is the sphere set. What is a claim about the hull is
# the waterplane: the set's transverse second moment is 114.9 m^4 against the
# hull's own 123.3 and its longitudinal 1063.8 against 1152.2, both about the
# centre of mass. Soft by 7-8% in each, which is the safe direction.
#
# **Having an angle of vanishing stability is not the bug — every real boat has
# one.** Coming back from 30° and not from 60° would be correct behaviour. The
# defect the paragraph above was written about was having it at 40°, which is
# inside the range an ordinary sea reaches, and it came entirely from the
# r = 0.945 / y = -0.6 pair.
#
# The wind sweep says the same thing from the other side, re-measured on the
# corrected pontoons: at `speed = 40` — the schema takes 40 and refuses 41 —
# the sea it builds carries the boat through **36.5 m of vertical travel** and
# rolls her **2.7°** at worst, and she still never goes over; at 35 m/s that is
# 26.6 m and 2.2°. (Sampled every 300 ticks from 300 to 3900; the vertical
# range is the wave, not a sinking — at these wind speeds the trough really is
# thirty metres down.) On the twelve pontoons, the same sweep rolls her **10.8°
# at 40 and 7.4° at 35**, so the corrected set is three to four times steadier
# in a storm as well as six degrees later to capsize.
#
# On this tier there is no wind speed a scene can author that
# capsizes it — and part of the reason is that `loom_physics` has no windage
# term at all: wind reaches the hull only as wave slope, so there is no
# beam-on heeling force here at any speed.
#
# Reproducing that costs a workaround worth knowing: `loom sim --assert` reads
# x, y and z on a node and has **no orientation axis at all**. Roll is read by
# parenting a marker to the Boat at local (0, 0, 5) — under a rotation phi
# about X it lands at (0, -5 sin phi, 5 cos phi), so `atan2(-dy, dz)` recovers
# a signed angle over the full ±180° and can tell "righted" from "inverted",
# which a mast's tilt from vertical cannot. The markers belong in a throwaway
# copy, not in here.
#
# One reading that surprises: `events.submerged` fires **once**, in the drop
# transient around tick 20, and never clears. That pair is hysteretic — 0.6 of
# the *collider* under to go in, 0.3 to come out — and the collider is the
# hull's box, which is a little over half under at the designed waterline. So
# the boat is permanently latched "submerged" and it is telling the truth about
# the box. Do not write a game rule against it here.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "3d7f81a2-95c4-4e60-b1a8-6c2f0e9d4471"

[[asset]]
key = "hull_white"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_hull_white.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "navy"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_navy.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "deck_grey"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_deck_grey.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "wood_trim"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_wood_trim.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "glass"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_glass.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "metal"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_metal.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "gold"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_gold.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "orange"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_orange.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "red"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_red.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "box"
path = "box"

[[node]]
name = "Sea"

  [node.components.Environment]
  sun_direction = [0.34, 0.58, -0.74]
  sun_strength = 1.3
  sun_color = [1.0, 0.97, 0.90]
  ambient = 0.55
  sky_zenith = [0.16, 0.31, 0.55]
  sky_horizon = [0.66, 0.74, 0.80]
  fog_density = 0.0016
  fog_falloff = 0.05

  # Light air. Enough to give the surface texture and a little motion; far too
  # little to knock over nineteen metres of boat. If this hull capsizes here,
  # the water did not do it.
  [node.components.Wind]
  direction_degrees = 20.0
  speed = 3.5
  gustiness = 0.25
  turbulence = 0.15

[[node]]
name = "Water"
parent = "Sea"

  [node.components.WaterBody]
  kind = "ocean"
  surface_height = 0.0
  density = 1000.0
  drag = 2.0

# **JIB VI.** Bow is +X, up is +Y, origin on the waterline and centred
# fore-aft — the exporter was told to do that so the scene needs no offset to
# float it. Dropped from 0.4 m so the settle is visible as a settle rather
# than being true at tick zero; the entry is gentle enough not to be an impact.
[[node]]
name = "Boat"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [0.0, 0.40, 0.0] }

  [node.components.RigidBody]
  dynamic = true
  # **57.636 tonnes: the hull's own measured displacement below her designed
  # waterline** — `../prefabs/jib_vi.loom` has the provenance and the choice of
  # waterline. It replaced 43,776 kg, which was a ratio applied to a guess and
  # was 24% light.
  mass = 57636.0

  # **The collider is here for the inertia, not for the collisions.** Without
  # one the body defaults to a 2 x 2 x 2 box — eight cubic metres — and a
  # nineteen-metre hull with a crate's rotational inertia rolls like a barrel,
  # which would fail this scene for a reason that has nothing to do with
  # buoyancy. This is the hull's own box: 19.06 x 3.32 x 6.60.
  #
  # **It is the only box with any mass in it.** The deck a player walks on is
  # twenty-six more, further down this file, all built with zero mass so that
  # every stability number in the header above is still measured against this
  # one alone. Do not delete it and do not give the others weight.
  [node.components.BoxCollider]
  half_extents = [9.53, 1.66, 3.30]

  [node.components.Buoyancy]
  coefficient = 1.0
  # Defaults, stated rather than inherited, because these scenes are about
  # stability and the damping is half of that: an analytic wave field has no
  # dissipation of its own, so an undamped pontoon gains amplitude every wave
  # until the boat launches.
  damp_linear = 4.0
  damp_quadratic = 1.0

  # **Sixteen pontoons, copied verbatim from `../prefabs/jib_vi.loom`, which
  # is where they were solved and where the reasoning for them lives** — eight
  # equal-volume slabs of the hull's own section curve, one sphere pair per
  # slab reproducing that slab's displacement *and* its waterplane area, the
  # whole set shifted 2.2994 m forward so the centre of buoyancy lands on the
  # centre of gravity the engine puts at the node origin. Read that file's
  # header before changing anything here, and change both together.
  #
  # **Order is the sum order** — stern to bow, port then starboard — because
  # `loom_water::buoyancy::solve` sums in it and float addition is not
  # associative.
  # Slab 0.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-5.9109, 0.2683, -1.1518]
  radius = 1.3462
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-5.9109, 0.2683, 1.1518]
  radius = 1.3462
  # Slab 1.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-3.859, -0.0247, -1.2271]
  radius = 1.1859
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-3.859, -0.0247, 1.2271]
  radius = 1.1859
  # Slab 2.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-2.1885, -0.1309, -1.2711]
  radius = 1.1364
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-2.1885, -0.1309, 1.2711]
  radius = 1.1364
  # Slab 3.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-0.6905, -0.1917, -1.2959]
  radius = 1.1104
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-0.6905, -0.1917, 1.2959]
  radius = 1.1104
  # Slab 4.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [0.7407, -0.2109, -1.2908]
  radius = 1.1025
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [0.7407, -0.2109, 1.2908]
  radius = 1.1025
  # Slab 5.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [2.1844, -0.2104, -1.2481]
  radius = 1.1027
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [2.1844, -0.2104, 1.2481]
  radius = 1.1027
  # Slab 6.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [3.7571, -0.1602, -1.1291]
  radius = 1.1236
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [3.7571, -0.1602, 1.1291]
  radius = 1.1236
  # Slab 7.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [5.9667, 0.058, -0.7255]
  radius = 1.2278
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [5.9667, 0.058, 0.7255]
  radius = 1.2278


